# Leave both unset for local dev against `npm run mock` — the Vite dev server proxies
# /api and /ws to the bridge (see vite.config.ts), so no URL is needed and there is no
# CORS issue to work around.
#
# Set both when pointing at the real Pi on the BEMS LAN (Phase F). The Pi's Node-RED
# needs `httpNodeCors` enabled in settings.js for this case — see
# docs/bridge-contract.md §Deployment.
#
# VITE_BRIDGE_HTTP_URL=http://<pi-local-ip>:1880/api
# VITE_BRIDGE_WS_URL=ws://<pi-local-ip>:1880/ws/live

# --- Supabase (Analytics 7d/30d history — Phase 4) -------------------------
# The ANON/PUBLIC key only — safe to ship in the browser bundle because it's
# RLS-constrained (see supabase/schema.sql + supabase/phase4_anon_read.sql). Never put
# the service-role key here; that one belongs only in server/.env, read directly by
# server/ingest.mjs, never bundled. Leave both unset to keep Analytics on 24h-only
# (bridge-backed) history — the 7d/30d range options simply don't appear.
#
# VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
# VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# --- Weather (Overview cards) ----------------------------------------------
# Open-Meteo needs no API key, so nothing secret goes in the bundle. These only
# move the forecast off its default site (the MMSU CARE office, Batac City) and
# are all optional.
#
# VITE_WEATHER_LAT=18.0553
# VITE_WEATHER_LON=120.5646
# VITE_WEATHER_PLACE=Batac City

# --- Node-RED admin login (CLI deploy scripts + the proxy) -----------------
# Only needed if the target Node-RED instance has `adminAuth` configured — the
# same login you'd use for the Node-RED editor itself. Leave unset for an
# instance with no admin login. Never has a VITE_ prefix: this must never ship
# in the frontend bundle, only read by Node.js scripts directly.
#
# The PROXY needs these too, and reads them from server/.env, NOT from this file — see
# server/.env.example. Setting them only here leaves the enrolment wizard unable to tell
# which devices are already enrolled, and that failure is silent.
#
# NODE_RED_ADMIN_USER=
# NODE_RED_ADMIN_PASS=

# --- Tuya cloud (server-side only; never in the browser bundle) -------------------------
# From the Tuya IoT developer console, Cloud > Project > Overview.
# TUYA_ACCESS_SECRET grants full control of every device in the project. Treat it as the most
# sensitive value in this file: it reaches hardware directly and nothing scopes it.
# TUYA_REGION picks the data centre the project is bound to (cn | us | eu | in). The wrong one
# fails authentication rather than redirecting, which reads as a bad secret.
TUYA_ACCESS_ID=
TUYA_ACCESS_SECRET=
TUYA_REGION=us
